															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606105603" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;" >cybersqyd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1605537504 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Nov 2020 14:38</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4819997">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>(Immortal, but that doesn't really matter)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If them being immortal doesn't really matter, why are they immortal?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If too many merge though… It can have unintended consequences.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Like what?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The foundation discovers the merging properties of SCP-XXXX and decides to experiment with it. Soon they get greedy, and an entire facility is destroyed in a merging explosion.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'greedy' implies they can use this SCP for some kinda&#8230;benefit? But like, it's really not clear how they could 'get greedy' here or indeed why the merging could cause an explosion.</p>
<hr />
<p>At the end of the day, my feeling on this is it needs more narrative oomph. Containment breaches are pretty cliche and generally should be avoided unless you have a really interesting thing to do with them.</p>
<p>The idea of these things concentrating emotions and being able to project them is interesting however: it feels like this just&#8230;needs to go somewhere? Like, if the Foundation 'gets greedy' by e.g. using them to project a lot of say, depression on D-Class to prevent them rising up, or a like, gentle background of happiness to keep their researchers motivated say; and then for this to go somewhere. Maybe it's ethics committee review where they argue about whether it's morally acceptable or necessary; maybe it breaks in some manner and they decide not to; maybe there's some kinda nuance in how it works that reveals itself at the end to explain why this was a bad idea, idk.</p>
<p>The dimensional rift angle feels&#8230;iffy like you could do it but as a final act twist, it's too vague alone to have any kinda implications, and having it happen earlier in the story will make this feel like it has a huge focus shift and like it's two stories shoved together.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

